#!/usr/bin/env python3
# set-arm -- deterministic rest-timer arming (C12 structural forcing point).
#
# Splits the ARMING of the in-session rest-timer push stack out of the model's
# hands and into a script that a set write triggers automatically. The model
# kept writing "타이머 설정 완료" in chat while never actually running the nohup
# push commands (C12, recurrence of the C5/C6/C7/C9/C10 "prompt discipline
# fails" class). This wires the timer to the execution decision-point (RULES
# dec-094): logging a set arms the timer, as one script action -- the model
# cannot skip it.
#
# Two-channel rest timer (DGN-594, owner-confirmed 2026-07-27):
#   CHANNEL 1 (quiet, always on a continuation rest): ONE live countdown
#     message the bridge edits in place every 10s -- remaining time + a
#     draining progress bar. Editing = no re-notify. Armed here by dropping a
#     control file the bridge CountdownDriver consumes (see emit_countdown);
#     the drift-snap + editMessage machinery is the framework-owned primitive, used
#     via its documented cross-process interface (this script never touches
#     bridge code).
#   CHANNEL 2 (separate notifying pushes): the milestone before/start alerts
#     below -- each a NEW message, so it rings. Coaching tips ride the 60s/30s
#     markers. Both channels coexist: one live timer + the milestone pushes.
#
# Deterministic parts (ALWAYS fired by this script, no model involvement):
#   - CHANNEL 1 live countdown control file for the completed exercise's
#     designated rest (continuation only)
#   - every 30s-grid before-marker (`⏱ N초 전`) for the completed exercise's
#     designated rest (from rx-calc resolve_rest_sec, authoritative)
#   - the end (set-start) marker (`🏋️ 세트 시작 — <next spec>`)
#   - the 15-min water reminder, ridden on the 60s marker when due, as a NATURAL
#     full sentence VARIED across sets (#35 -- WATER_LINES, not a "물 한 모금"
#     fragment). Code SoT: the model never has to compose the water line.
#   - a coaching tip on the 30s marker ONLY when the model passes --tip
#     (SPEC 5.10: the #36 default-on canned-tip bank is RETIRED -- no --tip
#     means a bare marker, never an invented one).
# Context parts (best-effort, passed in by the model as optional args):
#   --next   next-set spec text for the set-start marker (weight/RIR/reps).
#            omitted -> a generic set-start line still fires (timer guaranteed).
#   --tip    a form-correction tip to ride the 30s marker (a REAL observed form
#            issue / owner-just-raised carve-out; SKILL.md decides when to pass
#            it). Omitted -> NO tip rides at all (SPEC 5.10).
#   --tip60  an extra line to ride the 60s marker (rides after the water text).
#
# Fire model (mirrors workout-session SKILL.md "dynamic 30s-grid"):
#   - completed set is NOT the exercise's last working set -> CONTINUATION:
#     full 30s grid + end marker (rest between sets of the SAME exercise).
#   - completed set IS the last working set (set_seq >= prescribed sets) ->
#     TRANSITION: single set-start alert only, no before-markers.
#   - no next slot / session end / owner stopped the exercise EARLY -> pass
#     --no-next. Since C12-B (2026-08-24) this kills the ENTIRE inter-set rest
#     stack -- countdown + before-markers + set-start push -- not just the push.
#     Absent that, a generic set-start still fires after the rest.
#
# C12-B ARM-TRIGGER BINDING (owner live 2026-08-24, recurring complaint "a rest
# timer arms after my LAST set / with no set recorded at all"). ONE boolean,
# `rest_ok`, now decides the whole inter-set rest stack (countdown +
# before-markers + rest-over push + the REST_ARMED signal), and every term in it
# is read from DATA by THIS script:
#     rest_ok = not transition   # a further working set exists (rx-prescribed
#                                # count -- the last-set verdict, F14)
#           and not is_rung      # a warm-up/ramp rung is not a rest boundary
#           and rest_bound       # a real set WRITE triggered this arm
#           and not session_end  # the derivation says nothing follows
#           and not --no-next    # model may only ADD suppression
# `--no-next` is the ONLY model term and it can only SUBTRACT a rest, never be
# required to prevent one -- the outcome is identical whether or not the model
# cooperates. Two holes this closes, both observed live in session 10091:
#   * 08:37:39 gen=15 -- a full continuation stack fired while workout_sets had
#     NO row for that exercise (first row 08:41:53). QUOTED EVIDENCE -- the push
#     text actually emitted (verbatim, not prose, do not translate):
#         "✅ 60초 휴식 완료 🏋️ 세트 시작 — 로프 삼두 푸시다운"
#     set-arm is a plain CLI, so "a set was
#     recorded" and "a timer was armed" were independent ledgers. -> rest_bound
#     (set_write_bound(): row must exist AND be the session's newest write).
#   * 07:20:23 gen=4 -- the owner ended incline_dumbbell_press at set 3 of a
#     prescribed 4, so the count-based verdict legitimately said CONTINUATION.
#     --no-next was the only escape and it silenced ONLY the set-start push
#     while the countdown + before-markers ran anyway. -> --no-next now kills
#     the entire stack.
#
# H16 SINGLE-SOURCE REST DURATION (2026-08-24 -- the timer and the push
# disagreed on the same rest). ONE rest has ONE duration and ONE deadline. rest_checkpoints() is
# the ONLY place that turns the prescribed rest into times, and EVERY consumer
# -- channel-1 countdown, the 60s/30s before-markers, the rest-over push, the
# CONFIRM_FMT_REST confirm text, REST_ARMED, the DGN-918 idrill payload -- reads
# its value out of the dict it returns. No call site may subtract an offset of
# its own. The retired 10s REST_ROUNDTRIP_SEC correction was applied to the PUSH
# track only, so the same rest carried two durations (125 / 135) -- see
# rest_checkpoints' docstring for the decision and its grounds.
#
# Usage:
#   set-arm <session_id> <exercise_name> <set_seq> \
#           [--next "<spec>"] [--tip "<30s tip>"] [--tip60 "<60s line>"] \
#           [--no-next] [--db PATH] [--program PATH] [--today YYYY-MM-DD] \
#           [--dry-run]
#
#   set-arm countdown <seconds|range> --trigger <start|ready|set_done>
#                                      [--label TEXT] [--dry-run]
#     F22/F15 press-start HOLD countdown -- fires the SAME channel-1 countdown
#     primitive for a timed hold (isometric/hold working set, prep drill,
#     cool-down stretch) of N seconds. Decoupled from set logging: the model
#     runs it when the owner taps 시작 (start). A range like "20~30" uses the
#     upper bound. Arming cancels any prior live countdown (rest or hold).
#     #22 INTENT GUARD: --trigger is REQUIRED and must be a real execution
#     trigger (start/ready/set_done). A PLANNING utterance ("~하자/~할게") is not
#     a trigger -> the countdown is REFUSED (GUARD_BLOCKED, exit 3). This is the
#     arming DECISION guard, not the bridge renderer (countdown.py is untouched).
#
# --dry-run prints the push commands (with their sleep delays) instead of
# spawning them -- used by tests so no real Telegram push fires.
#
# Exit codes:
#   0 = armed (or dry-run printed)   -- also 0 on graceful degrade
#   1 = bad args only
# Arming is BEST-EFFORT on top of the set write: set-add calls this AFTER the
# DB write + SET_LOGGED line, and swallows any failure, so a bug here can never
# lose a logged set. This script therefore degrades (fallback rest) rather than
# hard-failing whenever it can.
#
# stdlib only: sys, os, json, argparse, subprocess, time.

