                                  args.exercise_name, override)["basis"]):
            print(line)
    for line in out:
        print(line)
    return 0


def rest_checkpoints(designated_rest):
    """H16 SINGLE-SOURCE REST SCHEDULE -- the ONE place a prescribed rest turns
    into times. Every consumer reads a NAMED key out of this dict; nobody
    subtracts an offset at their own call site.

    THE DEFECT THIS SHAPE KILLS (2026-08-24 -- the timer and the push
    disagreed on the same rest): the 10s REST_ROUNDTRIP_SEC
    correction was applied to the PUSH track only (`eff = d - 10`), while
    emit_countdown() was armed with the raw `d`. On a 135s rest the bar still
    read 0:15 when the push had already shipped the rest-over confirm
    (CONFIRM_FMT_REST at 135s). One rest, two durations (125 / 135) -- the
    same "two ledgers" pathology as C12-B's arm-vs-write split.

    DECISION -- both tracks share ONE value, and that value is the DESIGNATED
    (prescribed) rest. Grounds, in order of weight:
      1. A lead cannot be SHARED, by construction. Channel 1 is not a push: the
         bridge CountdownDriver is DEADLINE-anchored and self-corrects drift, so
         it can only ever render true remaining time. A lead channel 1 cannot
         structurally honor re-splits the ledger the instant it is non-zero.
         "Give both sides the same eff" therefore resolves to "give both sides
         the designated rest".
      2. Shifting the whole rest to d-10 (both tracks read 125) would make the
         timer and the push agree with each other while disagreeing with the
         PRESCRIPTION ledger -- rx-calc resolve_rest_sec, the set-table row and
         the DGN-388 rest-grounds fold all say 135. That trades H16 for the same
         pathology one seam over.
      3. The before-markers settle it. A marker is LABELLED with its own
         remaining time (rendered "⏱ 60초 전" = "60s to go"). Fired at
         `eff - 60` it reached the owner while
         the bar read 1:10 -- the label was simply false. Anchored to the
         designated deadline, the label is exactly what the bar shows.
    -> PUSH_LEAD_SEC is 0, the rest is `designated`, and countdown end, marker
       labels and rest-over push all land on the same instant.

    The lead SEAM is kept named rather than deleted so a future latency
    correction cannot return as an anonymous subtraction in one branch: it is
    returned as `push_lead` and applied in exactly one expression below.

    Returns (all seconds, all derived from `designated`):
      designated    -- the prescribed rest. EVERY owner-facing number and every
                       ledger line (confirm text, REST_ARMED, idrill payload).
      countdown_sec -- CHANNEL 1 live bar length  (end_sleep - bar_lead; the
                       bar starts bar_lead late, so it ENDS with the push)
      bar_lead      -- bridge pickup lead of the bar (BAR_PICKUP_LEAD_SEC)
      end_sleep     -- CHANNEL 2 rest-over push sleep       (designated - lead)
      markers       -- [{'remaining', 'sleep'}], sleep = end_sleep - remaining,
                       ascending sleep (soonest first)
      push_lead     -- the transport lead actually applied (0)
      effective     -- alias of end_sleep, kept for the DRY-RUN diagnostic line
    Grid rule cross-ref: rx-calc rest_checkpoints (DGN-388). That copy is
    UNCALLED and still carries the retired 10s offset -- set-arm is the live SoT
    for the rest schedule; do not re-mirror the offset back in from there."""
    d = int(designated_rest or 0)
    end_sleep = max(d - PUSH_LEAD_SEC, 0)   # channel 2: THE one lead expression
    # channel 1: the bar starts BAR_PICKUP_LEAD_SEC after the arm (bridge
    # pickup), so it runs that much shorter and ends with the push (item 9).
    bar_lead = BAR_PICKUP_LEAD_SEC if end_sleep > BAR_PICKUP_LEAD_SEC else 0
    countdown_sec = end_sleep - bar_lead
    count = end_sleep // 30
    markers = []
    for k in range(1, count + 1):
        remaining = 30 * k
        if remaining >= d:
            continue
        # F13: before-markers are capped to the 60s and 30s marks only
        # (at most two). Marks >= 90s ahead are suppressed -- they add noise
        # without value. If the rest is < 60s, only the 30s mark survives.
        if remaining not in (60, 30):
            continue
        markers.append({"remaining": remaining, "sleep": end_sleep - remaining})
    markers.sort(key=lambda m: m["sleep"])
    return {"designated": d,
            "countdown_sec": countdown_sec,
            "end_sleep": end_sleep,
            "effective": end_sleep,
            "markers": markers,
            "push_lead": PUSH_LEAD_SEC,
            "bar_lead": bar_lead}


